WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.260
Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp; Tom briefly off-camera you
were just telling us the slogan about NASA

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.189
and I thought it relates to something that
was interesting at the beginning of A SPACE

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:12.410
PROGRAM documentary, about how you don’t
just do things well, it seems like you’re

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:18.160
a perfectionist and you insist on making sure
that it’s perfect? Tom Sachs:&nbsp; When we

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.260
say perfectionism, it’s often described
as an illness and that might be true but what

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.680
we say in the studio is ‘It won’t fail
because of me. It won’t fail because of

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.680
my part.’ That’s a great slogan for a
collective work and when we put a man on the

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:42.180
moon it was 35,000 people working on it. Each
one of them said if we don’t get there,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.960
it’s not because of something I didn’t
do and if everyone really agrees and believes

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.100
in that, then we can achieve these incredible
things. Film Courage:&nbsp; You’ve said before

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.421
you don’t take vacations, you don’t believe
in them. Therefore, do you ever feel your

00:00:55.421 --> 00:01:00.219
creative work feels stale because if you’re
totally nose to the grindstone and you believe

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:06.770
in making sure that every I is dotted and
T is crossed, how do you recharge? Tom Sachs:

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:12.000
That’s a great question.&nbsp; Stanley Kubrick
never took vacations and little is known about

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.330
how he f*cked off but he had to…we all have
to do things to regenerate ourselves.&nbsp; I’m

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:25.320
into really normal stuff like…eating…going
to the bathroom…sexual intercourse…sometimes

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:33.530
I like to go surfing, weightlifting and skateboarding.&nbsp;
But ultimately, the thing that I say I don’t

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:39.600
take vacations is that I am really fulfilled
by my work and I am very lucky that the thing

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:47.570
that I…or I should say that I am extremely
privileged to be able to work for passion

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:54.350
and do the things that are important to me.&nbsp;
Which means that there is a disconnect between

00:01:54.350 --> 00:02:00.060
things that are done for money.&nbsp; To me, the
biggest curse would be to be really well-paid

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.150
to do something that you didn’t love to
do because then you’d be trapped by the

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:10.590
abstract.&nbsp; Whereas doing things for love
or for passion means that it doesn’t matter

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:15.530
if you’re getting paid or not.&nbsp; It’s
a detail…very, very important detail, but

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.860
it means that you can just do what you believe
in and it’s important to always organize

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:26.599
life toward that. The Shakers had this expression
Do The Job As If You Had a Thousand Years

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:31.490
to Live Yet To Die Tomorrow. Film Courage:&nbsp;
Well I know people always say “Don’t turn

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:36.209
your hobby into a job.”&nbsp; Find the second
thing you love to do, because once it becomes

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:43.819
a job then it’s laborious and you feel somehow
hindered by it. Tom Sachs:&nbsp; I disagree completely.&nbsp;

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:50.150
Dean Kamen in SLINGSHOT which is a great documentary
about…Dean Kamen the inventor of the Segway,

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:54.959
but also the inventor of the computerized
Insulin pump and a lot of amazing bio tech

00:02:54.959 --> 00:03:02.800
stuff said “The Key to Success is Do What
You Love and Get So Good at it That You Can

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:10.021
Make a Living Doing It.”&nbsp; Now of course,
half the job is doing it, your job.&nbsp; The

00:03:10.021 --> 00:03:15.890
other half is bringing it to the world which
is communications, paying your taxes, it’s

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:21.310
doing interviews, writing and talking about
it, helping out, educate, teach and share.&nbsp;

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:26.959
It’s important…it’s not just working
by yourself in a garrett, it’s communication.&nbsp;

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:36.500
And the movies that we make, Van [Neistat]
and I are in way communication aspects of

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.569
the sculpture.&nbsp; They are built like…the
movies are built, just like sculptures.&nbsp;

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:50.720
They’re films so they show the flaws, they
are shot with a Canon T2i with a missing pixel

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.209
in the middle about two-thirds of the way
down from the top of the screen you will see

00:03:55.209 --> 00:04:01.950
one red dot.&nbsp; It’s almost always there
and I think we’re about to get a new version,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:11.430
like the T8i or something?&nbsp; I’m not sure.&nbsp;
But it’s the idea that we started making

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:21.329
movies when iMovie came out and then iMovie
started to become so difficult and not functional.

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:25.689
That is when we stopped for about three years
and now we’re using Final Cut Pro because

00:04:25.689 --> 00:04:28.080
it’s easy again.
Film Courage:&nbsp; What lenses did you use because

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.599
you had such great, intricate close-up [shots]
of drills and things like that? Tom Sachs:&nbsp;

00:04:32.599 --> 00:04:39.699
That’s the off-the-shelf Canon zoom lens.&nbsp;
I don’t even remember the model number of

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:47.119
it but it’s probably something like a 20
or 30 millimeter to zoom out to be somewhere

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:52.520
between 80 to 125.&nbsp; It’s a good, basic,
off-the-shelf one. &nbsp; It’s not even the

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.779
one with the red ring on it.&nbsp; It’s not
the good one.&nbsp; I think we bought one of those

00:04:56.779 --> 00:05:04.710
later when we had to get another T2i to take
a picture of our T2i for the credit sequence

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.939
in the SPACE PROGRAM.&nbsp; We needed a photo
of the camera so we had to buy a second one

00:05:07.939 --> 00:05:14.530
just to take up one photo of the first one…which
we then returned. Film Courage:&nbsp; So the entire

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:19.610
film is shot on a Canon T2i.&nbsp; Wow!… Tom
Sachs:&nbsp; It’s name is Baby Jesus and in

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:23.900
the credit sequence you’ll see that…in
the credit sequence shot…you’ll see the

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:33.619
camera is in it.&nbsp; There’s some iPhone footage
also, but nothing…there is nothing exotic.&nbsp;

00:05:33.619 --> 00:05:39.310
When we shoot still footage of the sculptures
those are all shot with like a…40 ninja

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:46.939
pixel Hasselblad digital back.&nbsp; Those are
always shot by Genevieve Hanson or Joshua

00:05:46.939 --> 00:05:51.220
White, those are stills and those are for
images you’ll see in books and on websites.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:55.719
Film Courage:&nbsp; What was your decision to
use the T2i?&nbsp; I mean I was thinking you used

00:05:55.719 --> 00:06:01.550
a Red camera or something.&nbsp; It’s beautiful…
Tom Sachs:&nbsp; It’s really what Van had…that’s

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:11.750
Van’s camera and what’s important about
the T2i to our team is that Van knows what

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:18.110
all the buttons do.&nbsp; And the Red has…it’s
probably a superior camera in nearly every

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:23.550
way except you have a huge liability because
it’s so expensive and the virtue of a cheap

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:31.809
camera is that it’s not an heirloom product.&nbsp;
It’s a consumable even though a camera is

00:06:31.809 --> 00:06:35.139
not typically understood as a consumable.&nbsp;
The films, the data cards, are considered

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:41.179
consumables.&nbsp; But when something is that
cheap you can take risks.&nbsp; You don’t have

00:06:41.179 --> 00:06:47.369
to obsess about it and I think a lot of filmmakers
and camera aficionados are more obsessed with

00:06:47.369 --> 00:06:52.340
the hardware than what it’s capturing and
if something is only $500 and you’ve got

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:57.999
a huge production ahead of you, it’s really
a relatively small part and that reversal

00:06:57.999 --> 00:07:03.860
of economics and power is what gives us the
freedom to make a movie like we did.&nbsp; It’s

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:10.059
a a bar-lowering affect.&nbsp; And, I can’t
underscore this enough, the knowing how to

00:07:10.059 --> 00:07:16.849
use the tool is the most important thing.&nbsp;
Not what the tool can do.&nbsp; As a sculptor

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:24.460
I made much better work with lower quality
tools that were in my hands that I know I

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:30.089
have a muscle memory with a more precise,
better tool because it’s part of my body.&nbsp;

00:07:30.089 --> 00:07:33.400
I’m not thinking. So when Van is getting
these shots, he doesn’t have to worry about

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:42.219
learning about some great new feature.&nbsp; He
just goes and speed is one of the cornerstones

00:07:42.219 --> 00:07:52.649
of it.&nbsp; We have sort of ten rules of filmmaking
and rule number one is Finish the Movie. Film

00:07:52.649 --> 00:07:58.139
Courage:&nbsp; That’s a good rule. Tom Sachs:&nbsp;
And most movie makers don’t follow rule

00:07:58.139 --> 00:08:05.759
number one and most movies don’t get finished.&nbsp;
And so if you follow rule number one, that’s

00:08:05.759 --> 00:08:14.719
so much more important than rules number nine
through…ten.&nbsp; And we don’t always follow

00:08:14.719 --> 00:08:19.219
the other rules but we follow rule number
one. Film Courage:&nbsp; Why do you think rule

00:08:19.219 --> 00:08:23.999
number one is not always followed? What’s
the reason for that?&nbsp; Is it boredom…is

00:08:23.999 --> 00:08:28.270
it…? Tom Sachs:&nbsp; I don’t think it has
anything to do with filmmaking?&nbsp; I think

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:39.550
it’s just a question of character and will
and doing what it takes to get the job done.&nbsp;

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:46.440
I think it’s the bringing the ball to the
net metaphor, sport metaphor.&nbsp; And doing

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:56.220
what it takes to get it in the hole, like
whatever it takes.&nbsp; And I think before, when

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:06.949
you asked me about perfectionism.&nbsp; Perfect…is
the enemy of the good or something like that?&nbsp;

00:09:06.949 --> 00:09:11.620
This is not a perfectionist speaking saying
get the movie done because the movie is flawed

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:14.930
and I think if you look at A SPACE PROGRAM
you’ll see tons of flaws.&nbsp; There are all

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:23.730
these things that aren’t perfect about it.&nbsp;
All the individual sequences aren’t perfect

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:30.022
but it’s flawed, it’s got some character
flaws in that it’s maybe trying to do too

00:09:30.022 --> 00:09:36.080
many things or maybe it’s not long enough.&nbsp;
It would have been a perfect movie if it had

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.990
been about three hours long and we are shown
every little sequence in order and made industrial

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:44.680
films like we originally wanted to, but I
didn’t feel that was watchable.&nbsp; It would

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.529
be too alienating to the audience and there
are some great filmmakers who are perfectionists

00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:52.940
who make more perfect movies but it was important
to us to make something that people could

00:09:52.940 --> 00:10:01.790
see.&nbsp; But I love the films of avant-garde
sculptors who make these epic movies but they’re

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:09.470
really disrespectful to the viewer and they
don’t make it reasonable to finish the movie

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:15.339
and I think we’re living in a time where
you have to always make a choice whether what

00:10:15.339 --> 00:10:22.070
your expectation is of the viewer.&nbsp; Are you
trying to subject them to something or are

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:26.500
you trying to teach them something?&nbsp; Are
you trying to share or are you trying to help

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:31.310
them understand without thinking? I think
there are different kinds of movies for different

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:37.150
kinds of roles.&nbsp; We are really interested
in making movies that illustrate the aspects

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:43.231
of the sculptures that exist in time.&nbsp; In
other words this sculpture you know, which

00:10:43.231 --> 00:10:51.370
is maybe in a museum that maybe one time was
an African mask and it was used in a ritual

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:59.930
or this sword was used by a famous Samurai
or this sarcophagus was something that someone

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:10.259
was buried in…and all these, the mask, the
sword, the sarcophagus all had stories and

00:11:10.259 --> 00:11:13.900
events and things that occurred to them to
make them wonder.&nbsp; One was for a ritual,

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:18.930
one was for a fight,&nbsp; one was to house a
dead body.&nbsp; All those things are things that

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:25.029
existed in time and action so a lot of the
things, the sculptures or the movies about

00:11:25.029 --> 00:11:30.260
the sculptures show how these sculptors had
a history and part of a ritual activity that

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:38.250
was done in the studio.&nbsp; We go to other planets.&nbsp;
We explore them in our live demonstrations

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:44.110
and these movies show how we did that. Film
Courage:&nbsp; Of those remaining nine rules which

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:48.149
is the second most important besides number
one? Tom Sachs:&nbsp; Well, they are all really

00:11:48.149 --> 00:11:51.459
mundane things but they’re important.&nbsp;
And I’m not going to get them all and I’m

00:11:51.459 --> 00:11:59.310
not going to get them in order but they are
things like write the end of the movie before

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:05.370
you start shooting.&nbsp; Don’t try and start
shooting without having the end of the story

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:10.689
figured out because the third act is the hard
one.&nbsp; You have to understand that before

00:12:10.689 --> 00:12:16.769
you even start because if you don’t you
wind up like that amazing guy Francis Ford

00:12:16.769 --> 00:12:19.819
Coppola in Hearts of Darkness (A Filmmaker’s
Apocalypse 1991 documentary film) where he’s

00:12:19.819 --> 00:12:23.839
sitting in the Philippines in a trailer in
the middle of the night, typing all night

00:12:23.839 --> 00:12:28.649
and shooting all the next day and not sleeping
and that’s where everything falls apart.&nbsp;

00:12:28.649 --> 00:12:34.509
I think he got lucky that he got to finish
that movie.&nbsp; And though arguably it is his

00:12:34.509 --> 00:12:42.629
greatest film and he’s one of the world’s
greatest film directors, I think it was lucky

00:12:42.629 --> 00:12:48.000
that it was a great film.&nbsp; I don’t see
all those late nights being the answer.&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:52.740
I think a lot of things came together in kind
of a miraculous way.&nbsp; That’s an anomaly.&nbsp;

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:56.769
So that’s a situation you want to avoid.&nbsp;
And I think if you look at the history of

00:12:56.769 --> 00:13:01.990
his life, that was not necessarily a successful
feeling in time.&nbsp; It was years later that

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:08.449
it became [successful]…I think it was viewed
potentially as a failure in its time and then

00:13:08.449 --> 00:13:13.850
after some time became one of the greats.&nbsp;
Anyway, certainly an unhappy thing and that’s

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:22.839
something that we’re not looking to repeat.
[Another tip]&nbsp; Avoid camera movement. Film

00:13:22.839 --> 00:13:29.721
Courage:&nbsp; Especially with a DSLR like a T2i?
Tom Sachs:&nbsp; Any camera, whether it’s a

00:13:29.721 --> 00:13:38.910
DSLR or a red or any camera.&nbsp; Camera movement
is a privilege.&nbsp; It’s not a right.&nbsp; It’s

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:47.290
an indulgence…you should do as little work
as you can…an idea shouldn’t be anymore…Einstein

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:52.199
said this….and [pauses to think] I’m going
to screw this up…everything should be as

00:13:52.199 --> 00:13:58.350
simple as possible but no more so.&nbsp; In other
words, use a tracking shot.&nbsp; Zoom in, but

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:03.579
only to an effect or a better example may
be omit unnecessary words [referring to Authors

00:14:03.579 --> 00:14:09.720
Strunk and White].&nbsp; A sculpture should be
as small as it can be to communicate the idea.&nbsp;

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:13.579
That might mean it needs to be the size of
a building in some cases but it could fit

00:14:13.579 --> 00:14:19.329
in the palm of your hand.&nbsp; Everything else
is just hubris and ego. Film Courage:&nbsp; 2016

00:14:19.329 --> 00:14:25.160
– we’re filled with franchises and sequels.&nbsp;
Why should people care about A SPACE PROGRAM?

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:36.110
Tom Sachs:&nbsp; [stunned]…o.k….(laughing)
Film Courage:&nbsp; One word (laughing). Tom Sachs:&nbsp;

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:44.060
[Pausing] homemade…and you the individual.&nbsp;
We were here.&nbsp; In other words, I always wanted

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:50.800
my sculptures to be perfect like an iPhone
and I tried and once I even made a giant sculpture

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:58.430
that was nearly perfect but there are some
flaws and those flaws really drove me crazy

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:04.680
and once I kind of started to embrace those
flaws I began to understand that I could never

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:09.870
make something as perfect as an iPhone but
Apple could never make anything as sh*tty

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:14.610
as one of my sculptures.&nbsp; And when I say
sh*tty I mean showing the evidence a human

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:21.160
being was there. We’re at a time now where
you can have anything you want made perfectly

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:26.009
by robots.&nbsp; We’re getting there, you can
start to feel it.&nbsp; And the virtue of the

00:15:26.009 --> 00:15:32.319
handmade, the fingerprint, is being eliminated.&nbsp;
I think that is also why you see artisanal

00:15:32.319 --> 00:15:37.720
movements or you see a rise in ceramics.&nbsp;
In fact my ceramic bowls, when I push my finger

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:42.459
into the clay, there is a fingerprint that
will be there for 20,000 years after this

00:15:42.459 --> 00:15:46.860
whole building is turned to rubble and all
of our computers have returned to their inert

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:51.720
elements, we’re going to have a fingerprint
still there and ceramic still underneath all

00:15:51.720 --> 00:16:00.779
the rubble and shard. So I think with this
rise of robot-made stuff, you’re seeing

00:16:00.779 --> 00:16:09.660
a rise of artisanal made things.&nbsp; Slow food,
homemade bicycles, knives, clothes, you’re

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:15.619
even seeing people making their own cars from
scratch.&nbsp; It’s in a way a reaction against

00:16:15.619 --> 00:16:21.339
it.&nbsp; Zine culture is back as a reaction against
websites.&nbsp; Which I know websites work better

00:16:21.339 --> 00:16:28.240
than a zine anytime, but you don’t get to
hand it to someone.&nbsp; There is some sensuality

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:34.190
to the paper that you get to touch, burn it,
wipe your a$s with it, write a love letter

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:35.529
on it.&nbsp; It’s real.

